WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.228 --> 00:00:01.165 line:10%
Here's a puzzle:

2
00:00:01.215 --> 00:00:03.744 line:10%
a heart cell holds its own store of calcium —

3
00:00:03.794 --> 00:00:06.627 line:10%
so why does it need calcium from outside just to beat?

4
00:00:06.709 --> 00:00:08.351 line:10%
The answer is an amplifier.

5
00:00:08.587 --> 00:00:12.336 line:10%
An action potential opens the L-type calcium channels in the membrane.

6
00:00:12.395 --> 00:00:14.164 line:10%
Only a few ions enter.

7
00:00:14.329 --> 00:00:16.945 line:10%
They cross the gap and touch RyR2,

8
00:00:16.995 --> 00:00:19.687 line:10%
the release channel on the sarcoplasmic reticulum —

9
00:00:19.737 --> 00:00:22.155 line:10%
and the L-type channel closes behind them.

10
00:00:22.624 --> 00:00:26.590 line:10%
RyR2 opens, and the store pours out into the cytosol.

11
00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:30.370 line:10%
A handful of ions in; dozens out.

12
00:00:30.523 --> 00:00:34.012 line:10%
That is calcium-induced calcium release —

13
00:00:34.378 --> 00:00:36.857 line:10%
and it's what makes

14
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:38.936 line:10%
the heart contract.

15
00:00:39.469 --> 00:00:41.814 line:10%
To relax, the calcium has to go.

16
00:00:41.995 --> 00:00:44.199 line:10%
SERCA pumps most of it back into the store,

17
00:00:44.249 --> 00:00:45.986 line:10%
burning ATP to do it.

18
00:00:46.061 --> 00:00:48.488 line:10%
The sodium-calcium exchanger clears the rest —

19
00:00:48.538 --> 00:00:50.980 line:10%
three sodium in for every one calcium out.

20
00:00:51.455 --> 00:00:52.768 line:10%
Now the useful part.

21
00:00:52.897 --> 00:00:55.298 line:10%
That same L-type channel does a second job.

22
00:00:55.461 --> 00:01:01.189 line:10%
In the SA and AV node there are almost no fast sodium channels — so there, calcium doesn't trigger the beat.

23
00:01:01.255 --> 00:01:02.323 line:10%
It carries it.

24
00:01:02.601 --> 00:01:06.397 line:10%
In atrial fibrillation the atria fire five hundred times a minute,

25
00:01:06.447 --> 00:01:09.746 line:10%
and the AV node is the only electrical door to the ventricle.

26
00:01:10.219 --> 00:01:12.163 line:10%
Verapamil and diltiazem —

27
00:01:12.213 --> 00:01:15.262 line:10%
the non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers —

28
00:01:15.312 --> 00:01:17.209 line:10%
block that L-type channel.

29
00:01:17.627 --> 00:01:18.940 line:10%
Conduction slows,

30
00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:20.779 line:10%
the refractory period lengthens,

31
00:01:20.829 --> 00:01:22.371 line:10%
fewer impulses get through,

32
00:01:22.421 --> 00:01:24.339 line:10%
and the ventricular rate falls.

33
00:01:24.389 --> 00:01:27.929 line:10%
But the working myocyte uses that same channel to set its force.

34
00:01:27.979 --> 00:01:30.089 line:10%
Block it there and the squeeze weakens —

35
00:01:30.139 --> 00:01:32.739 line:10%
which is why these drugs are dangerous in a failing heart,

36
00:01:32.789 --> 00:01:35.315 line:10%
and in pre-excited atrial fibrillation.

37
00:01:35.784 --> 00:01:38.716 line:10%
It doesn't stop the fibrillation. It just narrows the door.

38
00:01:38.963 --> 00:01:40.450 line:10%
One last piece.

39
00:01:40.787 --> 00:01:42.510 line:10%
SERCA doesn't run free.

40
00:01:42.947 --> 00:01:46.979 line:10%
A small protein called phospholamban sits on it and holds

41
00:01:47.072 --> 00:01:49.439 line:10%
it back — that's the resting

42
00:01:49.518 --> 00:01:51.430 line:10%
brake on relaxation.

43
00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:54.274 line:10%
Phosphorylate phospholamban, and it lets go.

44
00:01:54.324 --> 00:01:55.867 line:10%
SERCA speeds up,

45
00:01:55.917 --> 00:01:57.917 line:10%
calcium clears faster,

46
00:01:57.968 --> 00:01:59.374 line:10%
and the heart relaxes faster.

47
00:01:59.501 --> 00:02:01.239 line:10%
That's lusitropy.

48
00:02:01.424 --> 00:02:03.531 line:10%
And one signal does all of it.

49
00:02:03.581 --> 00:02:06.930 line:10%
Adrenaline hits the beta-one receptor, cAMP rises,

50
00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:08.361 line:10%
and PKA goes to work —

51
00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:11.153 line:10%
on three targets. cAMP steepens the

52
00:02:11.203 --> 00:02:12.891 line:10%
pacemaker current,

53
00:02:12.993 --> 00:02:15.880 line:10%
so the heart beats faster. PKA phosphorylates

54
00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.542 line:10%
the L-type calcium channel,

55
00:02:18.592 --> 00:02:21.007 line:10%
so more calcium enters and the squeeze is stronger.

56
00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:22.845 line:10%
And PKA phosphorylates

57
00:02:22.895 --> 00:02:25.390 line:10%
phospholamban, so the heart relaxes faster.

58
00:02:25.461 --> 00:02:26.449 line:10%
Faster,

59
00:02:26.678 --> 00:02:27.669 line:10%
harder,

60
00:02:27.784 --> 00:02:30.511 line:10%
quicker to relax — one signal.

61
00:02:31.039 --> 00:02:32.565 line:10%
Sympathetic drive turns it up.

62
00:02:32.615 --> 00:02:34.920 line:10%
Verapamil turns it down.

63
00:02:35.011 --> 00:02:37.797 line:10%
And when the third arm fails and the

64
00:02:37.847 --> 00:02:39.062 line:10%
heart can't relax,

65
00:02:39.521 --> 00:02:43.561 line:10%
filling fails. That's the diastolic dysfunction of HFpEF.

66
00:02:44.032 --> 00:02:45.414 line:10%
So, to recap.

67
00:02:45.501 --> 00:02:46.570 line:10%
Cardiac muscle,

68
00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:47.845 line:10%
unlike skeletal,

69
00:02:47.895 --> 00:02:50.011 line:10%
needs extracellular calcium:

70
00:02:50.094 --> 00:02:55.239 line:10%
influx through L-type channels triggers a much larger release from the SR. SERCA and the

71
00:02:55.289 --> 00:02:58.595 line:10%
sodium-calcium exchanger clear it again. That same L-type

72
00:02:58.741 --> 00:03:02.123 line:10%
channel carries the upstroke in the AV node —

73
00:03:02.173 --> 00:03:06.935 line:10%
which is why verapamil and diltiazem slow the ventricular rate in atrial fibrillation.

74
00:03:07.171 --> 00:03:09.899 line:10%
And because the channel also sets contractile force,

75
00:03:09.949 --> 00:03:12.035 line:10%
the cost is negative inotropy:

76
00:03:12.143 --> 00:03:15.214 line:10%
avoid them in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction,

77
00:03:15.264 --> 00:03:17.737 line:10%
and in pre-excited AF.

78
00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.590 line:10%
And one more.

79
00:03:19.862 --> 00:03:22.169 line:10%
Sympathetic drive tunes

80
00:03:22.219 --> 00:03:24.292 line:10%
all three: cAMP speeds the node,

81
00:03:24.342 --> 00:03:26.510 line:10%
PKA opens more L-type channels,

82
00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:29.378 line:10%
and PKA takes phospholamban

83
00:03:29.455 --> 00:03:32.157 line:10%
off SERCA. Lose that last one,

84
00:03:32.207 --> 00:03:35.087 line:10%
and the heart can't relax — that's HFpEF.
